<h2>Build log<a href="#build-log">#</a></h2>
<p>I made a <a href="https://joshblais.com/blog/my-misadventures-in-soldering/">post</a>/<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A9zDIdh5GVA">video</a> about my soldering experience, and due to buying a “legacy” iron, I had a not super fun time in trying to get the joints to meld with the conical tip of my Weller WE1010 station. I ended up sending it back and getting a <a href="https://amzn.to/4wklLxD">JBC compatible USB-C iron instead</a>.</p>
<p>I would say that soldering is not the hardest thing in the world, but it did take some troubleshooting. That’s why I got a multimeter, to track traces and see where I went wrong. Visually, I could see one crossed connection on one of my Helios microcontrollers that would cause it to infinitely bootloop.   I plugged both halves into my computer, flashed QMK, and realizied none of my keys were being recognized…</p>
<p>What I actually forgot to do was close the solder pads on the bottom of the keyboard.  So, I took it back to the garage and 5 minutes and some <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wUyetZ5RtPs">drag soldering</a> later, I was rolling!</p>
<p>With all keys being recognized, I could tell that the “w” key as a cold joint as it wasn’t firing very nicely, so I walked out to the garage and reflowed the joint in 20 seconds. Other than that, the board appears to be working flawlessly a couple days on.</p>
<p><a></a></p>
<h2>Thoughts<a href="#thoughts">#</a></h2>
<p>So far, the switches and keycaps are fantastic. I love the silence, the speed is just as good as the choc, perhaps slightly better as there is more “springyness” to my keystrokes with the 40g of the TTC Frozen Silents.</p>
<p>I configured QMK strictly with C and no visual editor, which is a bit of a departure from my ZMK days and <a href="https://nickcoutsos.github.io/keymap-editor/">Nick Coutsos</a>’s keyboard editor.  I had to figure out homerow mods, with the clanker I was able to time them properly without much issue. I ran into a hiccup regarding <a href="https://www.usevia.app/">VIA</a>, as it was blocking my parenthesis from properly getting pressed, so I just removed it and went full (C)had.</p>
<p>Will I get rid of my chocofi? No, I think it’s a pretty good travel board without the wires, but I find the wired connection to be slightly more responsive and less fiddly (I’ve run into bluetooth connection issues in the past). With this board, I can just plug it in and go.  From a security perspective, wired boards are superior as well, so if that’s a consideration for you, I’d recommend the wire.  Get yourself some of these <a href="https://amzn.to/4pVOtCF">USB-C magnet connectors</a>, they’re pretty comfy.</p>
<p>Is this then “endgame of endgame keyboards” for me? Only time will tell. All I know now is that soldering is not some illusive mystical practice, so I think there will be many more projects in the future.</p>
